+### 1. Persistent W8A8 MoE layerlet + prefix-offset path
+Kernels and Python glue are substantially in tree (`csrc/xpu/moe_layerlet.cpp` adds `qwen36_moe_w8a8_middle_layerlet` and `qwen36_rows_per_expert_offsets_int64_out`; `xpu_moe.py` workspace reuse; resident int64 offsets scratch).
+Status (2026-06-15): synthetic middle-layerlet parity passes eager and XPU graph-replay (0.0 diff), and the new real-row/model-shaped TP4 decode gate also passes with `0` diff against rows-per-expert and offsets references. That clears standalone math, offset layout, skewed routing, and zero-row expert handling. The decisive timing trace still shows that the current middle-only layerlet is too narrow because it starts after remap/offsets/shared-expert prologue work, while the hot labels are `moe_forward_shared.custom_op`, `moe.quant_method_total`, `moe.shared_experts.apply_no_overlap`, and `qwen2_moe.shared.silu_and_mul`. Endpoint `fastgraph-exact-layerlet-smoke` is quality-clean in a shallow gate but slower (`88.283 tok/s`), and the prologue-inclusive endpoint path device-loses during PIECEWISE capture even with capture size `1`. Projection ceiling remains ≈ **172 tok/s** even if perfect across all 40 layers.
+Next: isolate the multi-rank/full-model prologue capture device-loss with a reduced TP4 reproducer, then make descriptors/scratch resident per layer/rank only after graph safety is proven. Potential: large as a floor-raiser (~150–170 tok/s base), **insufficient alone** for 200.
+
+### 2. Real XPU graph collectives (replace no-op comm capture)
+`VLLM_XPU_GRAPH_NOOP_COMM_CAPTURE=1` lets capture run but host-dispatches the real TP all-reduce. Removing it requires an XPU communicator graph-capture context (`parallel_state.graph_capture` currently asserts `CudaCommunicator`). Also de-risks P0a. Potential: medium-high.
+
+### 3. Native GDN decode stability
+Native GDN decode measures ~90–94 tok/s short-gate but corrupts under warm load (JSON @49, color @22). Trace shows selected layer-0 SSM state differs between good/bad requests at first decode step — a symptom of the P0a staleness class, not an independent root cause. Fix P0a first; native GDN then becomes safe. Potential: high but a stability rabbit hole until P0a clears.
+
+### 4. Graph replay metadata-copy reduction
+Every decode step pays Python overhead for metadata/block-table copies; the metadata-sanitation patch cost ~8 tok/s — proof this overhead is material. Move block-table updates into the captured graph or a single device-side copy; reuse static-shape metadata tensors. Potential: +5–15 tok/s.
+
+### 5. Custom all-reduce optimization
+Clone inputs (`VLLM_XPU_CUSTOM_ALLREDUCE_CLONE_INPUT=1`, `..._GRAPH_CLONE_INPUT=1`) cost bandwidth/latency for safety. Audit whether clones are still needed with PIECEWISE + native GDN prefill; tune launch grid/workgroup for Xe2. Potential: medium.
+
+### 6. TP↔PP / EP reshape
+TP4 all-reduces every layer; EP places experts across cards and moves routed activations. With 256 experts, EP is architecturally favored for c1. Validate `--pipeline-parallel-size 2 --tensor-parallel-size 2` and EP layouts under exact canaries. Potential: medium; invasive.
+
+### 7. Kernel fusion
+Unfused RMSNorm, residual add, activation, quant/dequant, RoPE. Fuse memory-bound chains (RMSNorm+residual+quant, SILU+mul+quant, RoPE+QKV). Potential: medium.
+
+### 8. Causal-conv1d / GDN attention kernel tuning
+Profile `csrc/xpu/gdn_attn/xe_2/chunk_causal_conv1d_xe2.hpp`; tune tiles/workgroup/scratch for B70; consider persistent-thread conv state in registers/SRAM. Potential: medium-high for native GDN paths.
+
+### 9. PIECEWISE boundary autotuning
+Split points are fixed; expose as knobs and sweep for the 512→512 decode workload. Potential: small-medium, low risk.
+
+### 10. Compilation-config / Inductor tuning
+`PIECEWISE` is coarse; explore XPU codegen/Triton tiling/fusion keys (avoid options that broke quality, e.g. async). Potential: small-medium.
+
+### 11. Weight layout / memory-format
+Audit `prepare_int8_moe_layer_for_xpu`; try oneDNN pre-packed / Xe2-cache-line-blocked layouts (quality-neutral repack at load). Potential: medium.
+
+### 12. Engine-core scheduler overhead
+For batch=1 decode the scheduler/core still run Python per step; move hot paths to C++, vectorize metadata, reduce EngineCore↔worker IPC. Potential: small-medium; more impactful at very high tok/s.
+
+### 13. Persistent / fewer kernel launches
+Persistent attention/MoE kernels carrying state across steps reduce launch overhead and improve cache locality. Potential: high but large effort.
+
+### 14. Compute/communication overlap
+Profile whether all-reduce is on the critical path; launch it earlier to overlap with subsequent compute (may require the #2 capture change). Potential: medium.
