+## R3. P1 layerlet — the synthetic test is blind; one cited flag is in the wrong tree
+
+Verified findings that change the layerlet work:
+
+- **The "0.0 max diff synthetic" proves only wiring, not correctness.** The layerlet (`csrc/xpu/moe_layerlet.cpp:169-194`) chains `cutlass_grouped_gemm_w8a8_int8_offsets_interface → silu_and_mul_quant_int8_xpu_out → cutlass_grouped_gemm_w8a8_int8_offsets_interface` — i.e. the **same three ops** as the offsets-based reference. Diffing layerlet-vs-offsets-reference is trivially 0. The real oracle is `ref_fused_moe` (`vllm_xpu_kernels/fused_moe_interface.py:1442-1589`) or the `rows_per_expert` path (`VLLM_XPU_W8A8_USE_OFFSETS=0`). **No in-repo test exercises the layerlet with real per-expert scales + skewed/zero-row routing.** Add that test before more endpoint A/B.
+- **`VLLM_XPU_INT8_MOE_MIXED_WORKSPACE` is NOT in vllm-xpu-kernels** (0 matches). It lives in **vLLM**: `vllm/model_executor/layers/fused_moe/experts/xpu_moe.py:34`. The resident `w8a8_offsets` scratch and workspace reuse therefore live in vLLM's `xpu_moe.py`, not the kernel. Any scratch-aliasing theory for the layerlet color-canary failure must be traced in vLLM, not assumed fixed by a kernels-rebuild.
+- **Two incompatible offset conventions ~150 lines apart** (`fused_moe_interface.py:597-601` oneDNN `[N]` end-only vs `:752-778` cutlass `[N+1]` leading-zero). The layerlet (`moe_layerlet.cpp:176,190`) and the offsets interface both **require** cutlass `[N+1]`. Assert the offsets tensor your harness builds is `[num_experts+1]`, int64, `offsets[0]==0`, `offsets[-1]==num_moe_inputs` — a wrong convention shifts every expert by one and reads OOB on the last expert (deterministic total corruption).
+- **int64→int32 truncation at the kernel edge.** `csrc/xpu/grouped_gemm/xe_2/grouped_gemm_xe2.hpp:142-144`: `pre_rows`, `gemm_m`, `cumsum_rows_for_experts` are `int` even in the offsets path. Safe at normal MoE `Total_M`, but this is the literal overflow hazard.
+- **Sparse-routing tile policy.** `grouped_gemm_xe2_interface.hpp:852` integer-divides `A_avg_M = A_total_M / num_experts`; for 256 experts + decode (Total_M=1), `A_avg_M≈0` selects `w8a16_policy_m_16` for the whole call regardless of per-expert skew — a tile/scheduler corner case that only real skewed routing exposes.
+- **No shared-expert code in this repo.** The shared expert (512 inter) is a separate MLP in vLLM's model layer; the layerlet only ever sees the 256 routed experts. A shared/routed scale mismatch cannot originate in `vllm-xpu-kernels`.
+
+Revised P1 first step: add the missing real-routing parity test (layerlet vs `ref_fused_moe` vs `rows_per_expert`, with real per-expert scales and zero-row experts) before any further endpoint promotion attempt.
